hey are diagonal. Can we cover them with a single rectangle? A rectangle covering both (1,2) and (2,1) would be the whole board (1-2 rows, 1-2 columns) which includes uncovered squares (1,1) and (2,2), not allowed. So we need at least 2 rectangles: one covering (1,2) and one covering (2,1). So answer = 2 = 2n-2 = 2. For the other permutation (1,2),(2,1) uncovered squares are at (1,2) and (2,1). Complement squares are (1,1) and (2,2). Similarly need 2 rectangles. So answer = 2.\n\nFor n=3, board 3x3. Let's test identity permutation (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) uncovered. Complement squares are all off-diagonal squares. Can we tile them with fewer than 2n-2 = 4 rectangles? The construction uses horizontal strips to right of each diagonal: row1: squares (1,2),(1,3) as one rectangle (1x2). Row2: (2,3) as 1x1 rectangle? Actually horizontal tile for row2: squares (2,3) (1x1). Row3: no right squares. Vertical strips below diagonal: column1: squares (2,1),(3,1) as 2x1 rectangle. Column2: square (3,2) as 1x1 rectangle. That's total 4 rectangles: H1 (1x2), H2 (1x1), V1 (2x1), V2 (1x1). Could we do with 3 rectangles? Let's try to combine some left and right squares across rows. For example, consider rectangle covering squares (1,2),(1,3),(2,3) - that's rows 1-2, columns 2-3. Does it include any uncovered squares? Uncovered squares are (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). The rectangle includes (2,2)? No, because columns 2-3 includes column 2, rows 1-2 includes row2, so (2,2) is inside rectangle (row2, col2). That's an uncovered square, not allowed. So can't include that. How about rectangle covering (2,1),(3,1),(3,2) - rows 2-3, columns 1-2. This includes (2,2) (row2, col2) which is uncovered. Not allowed. So maybe we cannot combine left and right squares across rows because of uncovered squares in the middle. However, maybe there is a different permutation that allows fewer rectangles. Let's test permutation (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Uncovered squares at (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Complement squares are all other 6 squ