\\\\[2mm]\n  &= (n-1)+\\sum_{j=2}^{n}|h_j-h_{j-1}|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the tiling problem is reduced to minimizing the “total variation’’  \n\n\\[\nV:=\\sum_{j=2}^{n}|h_j-h_{j-1}|\n\\]\n\nover all permutations \\((h_1,\\dots ,h_n)\\) of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Lower bound on the variation  \n\nFor any real numbers \\(x_1,\\dots ,x_n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=2}^{n}|x_j-x_{j-1}|\\ge |x_n-x_1|.\n\\]\n\nApplying this to the permutation \\((h_j)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nV\\ge \\max_j h_j-\\min_j h_j = (n-1)-0 = n-1.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (8) holds exactly when the sequence never “reverses direction’’; i.e. when it is monotone non‑decreasing or monotone non‑increasing.\n\nConsequently, from (7) and (8),\n\n\\[\nT\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the **identity permutation** \\(a_j=j\\) (the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((j,j)\\)).  \nThen \\(h_j=j-1\\) is strictly increasing, so the variation equals  \n\n\\[\nV=\\sum_{j=2}^{n}|(j-1)-(j-2)| = \\sum_{j=2}^{n}1 = n-1.\n\\]\n\nPlugging into (7) yields \\(T=2n-2\\).\n\nA concrete tiling:\n\n*For the upper region* (\\(i<j\\)):  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells  \n\n\\[\n(i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n).\n\\]\n\nThis is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle; there are \\(n-1\\) such tiles.\n\n*For the lower region* (\\(i>j\\)):  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering  \n\n\\[\n(j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j).\n\\]\n\nThis is a \\((n-j)\\times 1\\) rectangle; again there are \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\nThe two families are disjoint and together cover every square except the diagonal ones, exactly satisfying the problem’s requirement. Hence \\(2n-2\\) tiles suffice.\n\n---\n\n#### 7.  Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nBoth the lower bound (9) and the explicit construction give the same number of tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot202