overed squares is a *permutation matrix*.  \nThus there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis precisely the set of uncovered squares.  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_j=\\pi^{-1}(j)\n\\]\n\nbe the row of the uncovered square in column \\(j\\).  \nThe numbers \\(r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) we place at most two vertical tiles:\n\n* If \\(r_j>1\\) we place the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  T_j^{\\text{up}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le r_j-1\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which is a \\((r_j-1)\\times1\\) tile covering all squares **above** the uncovered square in column \\(j\\).\n\n* If \\(r_j<n\\) we place the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  T_j^{\\text{down}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  a \\((n-r_j)\\times1\\) tile covering all squares **below** the uncovered square in column \\(j\\).\n\nExactly one column has \\(r_j=1\\) (its \u201cup\u2019\u2019 tile is empty) and exactly one column has \\(r_j=n\\) (its \u201cdown\u2019\u2019 tile is empty).  \nConsequently the number of non\u2011empty tiles equals  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{up\u2011tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{down\u2011tiles}}=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThese tiles are pairwise disjoint, avoid all squares of \\(H\\), and together cover every square of the board except the uncovered ones.  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum}\\le 2n-2.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Critical cells  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS=\n\\Bigl\\{(r_j-1,j)\\mid r_j>1\\Bigr\\}\\cup\n\\Bigl\\{(r_j+1,j)\\mid r_j<n\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe first set consists of the cells directly **above** each uncovered square; the second set consists of those directly **below** it.  \nSince exactly one column has \\(r_j=1\\) and exactly one column has \\(r_j=n\\),\n\n\\[\n|S|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEvery cell of \\(S\\) must be covered by some tile, because the only uncovered squares are those of \\(H\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma  \n\n*Lemma.*  \nLet \\(R=I\\times J\\) be an axis\u2011parallel rectangle (with row interval \\(I\\) and column interval \\(J\\)) that contains **no** element of \\(H\\). Then \\(R\\) contains **at most one** element of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(R\\) contains two distinct cells of \\(S\\).\n\n1. **Both cells are above\u2011type.**  \n   They have the form \\((r_j-1,j)\\) and \\((r_k-1,k)\\) with \\(j\\neq k\\).  \n   Without loss assume \\(j<k\\).  \n   Since both cells lie in \\(R\\), we have \\(j,k\\in J\\) and \\(r_j-1,r_k-1\\in I\\).  \n   Because the rows in \\(I\\) form a contiguous interval, \\(I\\) contains every integer between \\(r_j-1\\) and \\(r_k-1\\).  \n   The permutation property gives \\(r_j\\neq r_k\\); assume \\(r_j<r_k\\). Then \\(r_j\\le r_k-1\\), so \\(r_j\\in I\\).  \n   Moreover \\(j\\in J\\). Hence \\((r_j,j)\\in H\\) lies in \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis.\n\n2. **Both cells are below\u2011type.**  \n   This case is symmetric to the previous one.\n\n3. **One above\u2011type and one below\u2011type.**  \n   Suppose the cells are \\((r_j-1,j)\\) and \\((r_k+1,k)\\).  \n\n   *If \\(j=k\\):* then \\(I\\) contains both \\(r_j-1\\) and \\(r_j+1\\); consequently it also contains \\(r_j\\). Together with column \\(j\\in J\\) we obtain \\((r_j,j)\\in H\\subseteq R\\), a contradiction.\n\n   *If \\(j\\neq k\\):* assume \\(j<k\\).  \n   Since \\((r_j-1,j)\\in R\\) and \\((r_k+1,k)\\in R\\), we have \\(j,k\\in J\\) and \\(r_j-1,r_k+1\\in I\\).  \n   The interval \\(I\\) therefore contains every integer between \\(r_j-1\\) and \\(r_k+1\\).  \n\n   - If \\(r_j\\le r_k\\), then \\(r_j\\) lies between \\(r_j-1\\) and \\(r_k+1\\); thus \\(r_j\\in I\\) and, because \\(j\\in J\\), \\((r_j,j)\\in H\\subseteq R\\).  \n   - If \\(r_j>r_k\\), then \\(r_k\\) lies between \\(r_j-1\\) and \\(r_k+1\\); thus \\(r_k\\in I\\) and, because \\(k\\in J\\), \\((r_k,k)\\in H\\subseteq R\\).\n\n   In either subcase an uncovered square belongs to \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis.\n\nAll possibilities lead to a contradiction; therefore a rectangle avoiding all uncovered squares can contain at most one element of \\(S\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEach tile is a rectangle that avoids all squares of \\(H\\); by the lemma it can cover at most one cell of \\(S\\).  \nSince every cell of \\(S\\) must be covered, any tiling requires at least \\(|S|\\) tiles.  \nUsing (2),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum}\\ge |S|=2n-2.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom (1) and (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum}=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\nThus Matilda can achieve the required condition with exactly \\(4048\\) rectangular tiles, and no arrangement with fewer tiles exists. \u220e"
