 is a function  \n\\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nThe column condition forces \\(\\sigma\\) to be a bijection; hence \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right parts  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR_i=\\{(i,j):\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set of all covered squares is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\;\\cup\\;\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) are disjoint, because a column lies either to the left or to the right of the unique uncovered square in its row.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Rectangles can be assumed left‑ or right‑justified  \n\nConsider a rectangle \\(T\\) that contains a cell of some \\(L_i\\).  \nLet the column interval of \\(T\\) be \\([c,d]\\) with \\(c\\le d\\).  \nSince \\(T\\) contains a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<\\sigma(i)\\), we have \\(d<\\sigma(i)\\).  \nFor any row \\(r\\) belonging to \\(T\\) we also have \\(d<\\sigma(r)\\); otherwise \\((r,d)\\) would be an uncovered square, contradicting the definition of a tile.  \nConsequently every row of \\(T\\) satisfies \\(L_r\\supseteq\\{(r,1),\\dots ,(r,d)\\}\\).  \nHence we may replace \\(T\\) by the rectangle \\([1,d]\\times\\) (the same rows), which is still contained in the left part and covers exactly the same cells.  \nThus **every rectangle covering left cells can be taken left‑justified** (its left side coincides with the left border of the board).  \n\nThe same sliding argument applied to a rectangle covering a cell of some \\(R_i\\) shows that it can be taken **right‑justified** (its right side coincides with the right border).\n\nTherefore any tiling of \\(\\mathcal C\\) consists of a collection \\(\\mathcal L\\) of left‑justified rectangles covering all \\(L_i\\) and a collection \\(\\mathcal R\\) of right‑justified rectangles covering all \\(R_i\\).  \nThe two collections are disjoint because a left‑justified rectangle uses only columns \\(\\le n-1\\) while a right‑justified rectangle uses only columns \\(\\ge 2\\); they never overlap.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the left side  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) put  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=|L_i|=\\sigma(i)-1 .\n\\]\n\nThe numbers \\(\\ell_i\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); in particular each positive integer \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) occurs exactly once as some \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nFix \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and let \\(i_k\\) be the unique index with \\(\\ell_{i_k}=k\\).  \nThe cell \\((i_k,k)\\) is the **rightmost** cell of the left segment of row \\(i_k\\).\n\n*Claim.* Any left‑justified rectangle that contains \\((i_k,k)\\) has width exactly \\(k\\).\n\nIndeed, a left‑justified rectangle has column interval \\([1,w]\\) for some width \\(w\\).  \nIf \\(w>k\\) then the rectangle would also contain the cell \\((i_k,k+1)\\), which lies to the right of the uncovered square \\((i_k,\\sigma(i_k))\\) and therefore is not allowed.  \nIf \\(w<k\\) the rectangle would miss the cell \\((i_k,k)\\).  \nHence \\(w=k\\).\n\nBecause the widths \\(k\\) are all distinct, the cells \\((i_k,k)\\) (one for each \\(k\\)) force **pairwise distinct** left‑justified rectangles.  \nConsequently any tiling of the left part needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles:\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|\\ge n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A lower bound for the right side  \n\nDefine the right‑segment lengths  \n\n\\[\nr_i:=|R_i|=n-\\sigma(i) .\n\\]\n\nAgain \\(\\{r_i\\}\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); each positive integer \\(k\\) occurs exactly once.  \nFor the unique row \\(j_k\\) with \\(r_{j_k}=k\\) the **leftmost**