oth an a_i and a b_j? The solution says each rectangle belongs either to left side or right side. Does that hold? The solution defines left side rectangles as those that contain only cells lying strictly to the left of the uncovered squares it meets. Similarly for right side. But is it possible for a rectangle to contain cells both left of some uncovered squares and right of others? For example, a rectangle that includes cells from two rows: in one row it may be left of that row's uncovered square, in another row it may be right of that row's uncovered square. But then the rectangle would cross the uncovered squares? Let's think: The rectangle is axis-aligned. If it contains cells from row i and row j, and the uncovered squares are at (i, sigma(i)) and (j, sigma(j)). The rectangle's columns are from some left column L to right column R. For the rectangle to contain cells left of uncovered squares in row i, we need R < sigma(i). For it to contain cells right of uncovered squares in row j, we need L > sigma(j). So we would need R < sigma(i) and L > sigma(j). Since L ≤ R, we need sigma(j) < L ≤ R < sigma(i). So sigma(j) < sigma(i). So if sigma(j) < sigma(i), then it's possible that a rectangle could contain cells left of uncovered squares in row i (i.e., columns left of sigma(i)) but also right of uncovered squares in row j (columns right of sigma(j)). But the rectangle would also contain the column interval between sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1. That interval includes the uncovered square at (j, sigma(j))? Actually the rectangle's rows include both rows i and j. The rectangle includes all cells in the rectangle region: all rows from i1 to i2 and columns L to R. If L > sigma(j) and R < sigma(i), then the rectangle does not include column sigma(j) (since L > sigma(j)), so it does not include the uncovered square at (j, sigma(j)). It also does not include column sigma(i) (since R < sigma(i)), so it does not include the uncovered square at (i, sigma(i)). So indeed the rectangle could be placed such that it lies strictly between sigma(j) and sigma(i) columns, covering cells left of uncovered squares in row i (since those are columns < sigma(i)) and right of uncovered squares in row j (since those are columns > sigma(j)). So the rectangle would be \"between\" the two uncovered squares. But the solution's classification of rectangles as either left-side or right-side might be incomplete: they assume each rectangle is either entirely left of the uncovered squares it meets, or entirely right of them. But a rectangle could be \"between\" uncovered squares in different rows, i.e., left of some and right of others. However, is that allowed given the requirement that each row and column has exactly one uncovered square? Let's examine.\n\nWe need to tile all cells except the uncovered squares. The uncovered squares are at positions (i, sigma(i)). The rest of the grid is the complement of these n cells. The complement is a union of two sets: cells to the left of the diagonal (i.e., j < sigma(i)) and cells to the right (j > sigma(i)). For each row i, cells left of sigma(i) are left side, cells right are right side. For each column j, cells above (i < sigma^{-1}(j)?) Actually column j's uncovered square is at row sigma^{-1}(j). So cells above that row are \"above\", cells below are \"below\". But the rectangle classification used is based on row perspective: left side or right side. But a rectangle could contain cells from multiple rows, some left, some right. However, if a rectangle contains cells from row i left of sigma(i) and row j right of sigma(j), then it must also contain the cell at (i, sigma(j))? Not necessarily; the rectangle includes all cells with rows between i and j (if i<j) and columns between L and R. Let's assume i<j. Then rectangle includes rows i..j and columns L..R. If L > sigma(j) and R < sigma(i), then sigma(j) < L ≤ R < sigma(i). Since sigma(j) < sigma(i), there is a gap between sigma(j) and sigma(i). The rectangle includes c