                    "reasoning_content": "We have a problem: \"Consider a 2025x2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nSolution: They set n=2025. They argue uncovered squares form a permutation matrix. They define left and right cells a_i and b_i. Lemma 1: rectangle left of uncovered squares can contain at most one a_i. Lemma 2: rectangle right of uncovered squares can contain at most one b_i. Since there are n-1 a_i and n-1 b_i, need at least 2n-2 tiles. Then they give a construction achieving 2n-2: identity permutation, uncovered squares are diagonal. They define vertical rectangles V_j for each column j (1 ≤ j ≤ n-1) covering cells below diagonal in column j, and horizontal rectangles H_i for each row i (1 ≤ i ≤ n-1) covering cells to the right of diagonal in row i. They verify that these are disjoint and cover all non-diagonal cells. So total tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2 = 4048. So answer is 4048.\n\nWe need to verify correctness, reasoning validity, and presentation (justification gaps or critical errors). The solution appears correct. Let's check each part.\n\nFirst, answer: 4048. Is that correct? Let's think about the problem: we need to cover all but one square per row and column. So we need to leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and column. That's exactly a permutation matrix of uncovered squares. So we need to tile the rest with rectangles (tiles). The solution claims lower bound 2n-2 and construction achieving it. Is that indeed minimal? Let's think: The lower bound argument uses a_i and b_i cells. There are n-1 a_i and n-1 b_i. Each rectangle can cover at most one a_i or b_i, but can a rectangle