 rectangle could be placed such that it lies strictly between sigma(j) and sigma(i) columns, covering cells left of uncovered squares in row i (since those are columns < sigma(i)) and right of uncovered squares in row j (since those are columns > sigma(j)). So the rectangle would be \"between\" the two uncovered squares. But the solution's classification of rectangles as either left-side or right-side might be incomplete: they assume each rectangle is either entirely left of the uncovered squares it meets, or entirely right of them. But a rectangle could be \"between\" uncovered squares in different rows, i.e., left of some and right of others. However, is that allowed given the requirement that each row and column has exactly one uncovered square? Let's examine.\n\nWe need to tile all cells except the uncovered squares. The uncovered squares are at positions (i, sigma(i)). The rest of the grid is the complement of these n cells. The complement is a union of two sets: cells to the left of the diagonal (i.e., j < sigma(i)) and cells to the right (j > sigma(i)). For each row i, cells left of sigma(i) are left side, cells right are right side. For each column j, cells above (i < sigma^{-1}(j)?) Actually column j's uncovered square is at row sigma^{-1}(j). So cells above that row are \"above\", cells below are \"below\". But the rectangle classification used is based on row perspective: left side or right side. But a rectangle could contain cells from multiple rows, some left, some right. However, if a rectangle contains cells from row i left of sigma(i) and row j right of sigma(j), then it must also contain the cell at (i, sigma(j))? Not necessarily; the rectangle includes all cells with rows between i and j (if i<j) and columns between L and R. Let's assume i<j. Then rectangle includes rows i..j and columns L..R. If L > sigma(j) and R < sigma(i), then sigma(j) < L ≤ R < sigma(i). Since sigma(j) < sigma(i), there is a gap between sigma(j) and sigma(i). The rectangle includes columns between sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1. In row i, those columns are left of sigma(i) (since sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1). In row j, those columns are right of sigma(j). So indeed the rectangle includes cells left of uncovered square in row i and right of uncovered square in row j. So it's a \"mixed\" rectangle. The solution's classification would not count such rectangles as left-side or right-side; they would be a third type. The lemmas only bound left-side rectangles covering a_i and right-side covering b_i. But a mixed rectangle could potentially cover both an a_i and a b_j, thus reducing the number of rectangles needed. The solution's lower bound may be too high if such mixed rectangles are possible.\n\nBut is it possible to have a rectangle that contains cells both left of some uncovered squares and right of others while still not covering any uncovered squares? Yes, as we described. For any permutation sigma, we can consider the region of the grid that is not covered by uncovered squares. This region is the complement of the permutation matrix. This region is a union of \"northwest\" and \"southeast\" regions relative to each uncovered cell. Actually, the complement is the set of all cells (i, j) with j != sigma(i). So it's everything except the diagonal of the permutation. This region is not convex; it's like a grid with holes at each (i, sigma(i)). The complement is not necessarily partitionable into left-only and right-only rectangles only; you could have rectangles that cross between rows.\n\nBut the solution's construction uses vertical rectangles V_j (covering cells below diagonal in column j) and horizontal rectangles H_i (covering cells to the right of diagonal in row i). Those are indeed left-only or right-only relative to each row's uncovered square: V_j covers cells left of diagonal for rows i>j (since sigma(i)=i, j < i, so column j is left of sigma(i)=i). So V_j is left-side rectangle. H_i covers cells right of diagonal for columns j>i, so right-side rectangle. So the construction uses only left-side and right-side rectangles. But the lower bound argument only considered left-side rectangles covering a_i and right-side covering b_i. However, could a rectangle be both left-side for some rows and right-side for others, thereby covering both an a_i and a b_j? If so, the lower bound would need to consider that each rectangle could cover at most one a_i and at most one b_i, but could cover both, which would reduce the total needed. But the lemmas only bound each rectangle type separately, not combined. They claim any rectangle belongs either to left-side or right-side. That is a claim that might be false. Let's examine the claim: \"Each rectangle of a tiling belongs either to the 'left side' (it contains only cells left of the uncovered squares it meets) or to the 'right side' (it contains only cells right of them).\" Is that true? Consider a rectangle that contains cells from rows i and j. If it contains a cell left of uncovered square in row i, then its rightmost column must be less than sigma(i). If it also contains a cell right of uncovered square in row j, then its leftmost column must be greater than sigma(j). So we need sigma(j) < leftmost column ≤ rightmost column < sigma(i). This is possible if sigma(j) < sigma(i). So the rectangle would be \"between\" sigma(j) and sigma(i). But does that rectangle contain any uncovered squares? No, because its columns are between sigma(j) and sigma(i), exclusive. So it does not contain any uncovered squares. So it is a valid rectangle. But does it contain cells left of uncovered squares in row i? Yes, because all its columns are less than sigma(i). Does it contain cells right of uncovered squares in row j? Yes, because all its columns are greater than sigma(j). So it contains both left and right cells relative to different rows. So the rectangle is not purely left-side or right-side. So the claim that each rectangle belongs either to left side or right side is false.