nal. The remaining cells are 6 cells. Could we tile them with 3 rectangles? Possibly: rectangle covering (1,1),(2,1) vertical column 1 rows 1-2, rectangle covering (1,3),(2,2),(3,2) maybe not rectangular. Let's systematically search small n to see minimal number of rectangles. For n=2, we have 2x2 grid, need each row and column have exactly one uncovered square. So uncovered squares must be a permutation: either (1,1),(2,2) or (1,2),(2,1). The remaining cells are 2 cells. How many rectangles needed? The two remaining cells are either both in same row or column? Let's check (1,1),(2,2) uncovered. Remaining cells: (1,2) and (2,1). They are not aligned; they cannot be covered by a single rectangle because they are not in same row or column. So need two rectangles (each 1x1). So minimal number is 2 = 2n-2 = 2. So for n=2, lower bound holds.\n\nFor n=3, we saw lower bound 4. Could we achieve 3? Let's try to find a tiling with 3 rectangles. The uncovered squares must be a permutation of size 3. Let's try permutation (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Then uncovered squares: (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). The remaining cells: all cells except these three. Let's list them: (1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,2),(3,3). Can we partition them into 3 rectangles? Let's try to see if we can group them: maybe rectangle covering (1,1),(2,1) vertical column 1 rows 1-2; rectangle covering (1,3),(2,2),(3,2),(3,3) maybe not rectangular. Let's think: rectangle covering (1,3),(2,2),(3,2),(3,3) is not rectangular shape. Let's try other grouping: rectangle covering (1,1),(1,3) horizontal row 1 columns 1 and 3, but not contiguous (need rectangle covering columns 1-3 includes column 2 which is uncovered at (1,2). So can't. So maybe we need more rectangles. So 4 seems minimal.\n\nBut we need to consider general n. The lower bound may be tight, but we need to verify that the claim \"each rectangle belongs either left side or right side\" is indeed true given the condition that each row and column has exactly one uncovered square. Let's examine more carefully: Suppose we have a rectangle R that contains cells left of uncovered squares in some rows and right of uncovered squares in other rows. Then for each row i that R intersects, either all columns of R are left of sigma(i) or all are right of sigma(i) (since R cannot contain sigma(i) because uncovered squares cannot be covered). So for each row i intersected by R, either R's column interval [L,R] is a subset of [1, sigma(i)-1] (left side) or a subset of [sigma(i)+1, n] (right side). So for each row i intersected, the rectangle is either left or right relative to that row. If R intersects rows i and j, and for i it's left side, for j it's right side, then we have sigma(i) > R (rightmost column) and sigma(j) < L (leftmost column). So sigma(j) < L ≤ R < sigma(i). So sigma(j) < sigma(i). So the uncovered squares in rows i and j are in different columns with sigma(j) < sigma(i). So the rectangle's column interval lies between sigma(j) and sigma(i). That is possible. However, does this rectangle intersect any uncovered squares in other rows? For any row k that R intersects, we need either sigma(k) > R or sigma(k) < L. So sigma(k) must be either > R or < L. So sigma(k) cannot be between L and R inclusive. So the rows intersected by R must have uncovered squares either all to the right of R (sigma > R) or all to the left of R (sigma < L). So the rectangle's rows are a set of rows whose sigma values are either all > R or all < L. So the rectangle's rows are either all on one side of the permutation in terms of column values. So the rectangle can be \"mixed\" only if the set of rows intersected includes some rows with sigma values less than L and some rows with sigma values greater than R. But that would mean there is a row with sigma < L and a row with sigma > R. However, the rectangle's rows must be contiguous (since rectangle includes all rows between min and max). So if we have a row with sigma < L and a row with sigma > R, then there must be 