e the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are\npairwise distinct, and similarly for the \\(R_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A left‑type rectangle contains at most one \\(L_i\\)\n\n*Lemma.* No left‑type rectangle can contain two different cells \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\)\n(\\(i\\neq j\\)).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a left‑type rectangle \\(R\\) contains \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) with\n\\(i<j\\).  Let the row interval of \\(R\\) be \\([p,q]\\) and the column interval be\n\\([c,d]\\).  Because \\(R\\) is left‑type, (1) holds, i.e.\n\n\\[\nd<\\min_{r\\in[p,q]}\\pi(r). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe cell \\(L_i\\) is either \\((i,\\pi(i+1))\\) or \\((i+1,\\pi(i))\\); in both cases its\ncolumn equals \\(\\min\\{\\pi(i),\\pi(i+1)\\}\\).  Denote this column by \\(c_i\\).\nSimilarly denote the column of \\(L_j\\) by \\(c_j\\).  Both \\(c_i\\) and \\(c_j\\) belong to\nthe column interval \\([c,d]\\), so \\(c_i,c_j\\le d\\).\n\nThe rows of \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) lie inside \\([p,q]\\); consequently the set\n\\(\\{\\pi(p),\\pi(p+1),\\dots ,\\pi(q)\\}\\) contains the four numbers\n\\(\\pi(i),\\pi(i+1),\\pi(j),\\pi(j+1)\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\nm=\\min\\{\\pi(i),\\pi(i+1),\\pi(j),\\pi(j+1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, the minimum \\(m\\) is attained at a **unique**\nrow, say \\(r^\\*\\in[p,q]\\).  Hence \\(\\min_{r\\in[p,q]}\\pi(r)=m\\).\n\nNow \\(c_i=\\min\\{\\pi(i),\\pi(i+1)\\}\\ge m\\) and \\(c_j=\\min\\{\\pi(j),\\pi(j+1)\\}\\ge m\\).\nAt least one of the two inequalities is strict (otherwise the same column would be\nassigned to two different rows, contradicting the bijectivity of \\(\\pi\\)).  Thus\n\\(\\max\\{c_i,c_j\\}>m\\).\n\nBut (3) gives \\(d<m\\), while \\(c_i,c_j\\le d\\).  Hence \\(\\max\\{c_i,c_j\\}\\le d<m\\),\na contradiction.  Therefore a left‑type rectangle cannot contain two distinct\n\\(L_i\\). ∎\n\nThe same argument, with the inequalities reversed, shows that a right‑type rectangle\ncannot contain two distinct \\(R_i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nAll left‑type tiles together must cover the \\(n-1\\) distinct cells \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\);\nby the lemma each left‑type tile covers at most one of them, so at least \\(n-1\\) left‑type\ntiles are necessary.  Similarly at least \\(n-1\\) right‑type tiles are necessary.\nSince a tile cannot be simultaneously left‑type and right‑type, any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. take \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all\n\\(i\\).  For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) put two tiles:\n\n* a horizontal tile covering row \\(i\\) and columns \\(i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\)\n  (size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\));\n* a vertical tile covering column \\(i\\) and rows \\(i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\)\n  (size \\((n-i)\\times1\\)).\n\nThese \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, none of them contains a diagonal\nsquare, and together they cover every off‑diagonal unit square exactly once.  Hence a\ntiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles coincide;\ntherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
