\\(b_i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAny axis‑parallel rectangle containing the two cells \\((i,\\pi(i+1))\\) and\n\\((i+1,\\pi(i))\\) must contain the whole sub‑grid\n\\(\\{i,i+1\\}\\times\\{\\pi(i),\\pi(i+1)\\}\\); consequently it also contains one\nof the diagonal holes \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) or \\((i+1,\\pi(i+1))\\), which is\nforbidden. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2  \nA rectangle cannot contain a cell from the sub‑grid \\(\\{a_i,b_i\\}\\) and a\ncell from a different sub‑grid \\(\\{a_j,b_j\\}\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\) contains a cell from sub‑grid \\(i\\) and a cell\nfrom sub‑grid \\(j\\) with \\(j>i+1\\) (the case \\(j<i-1\\) is symmetric).\nWithout loss of generality \\(R\\) contains \\(a_i=(i,\\pi(i+1))\\) and\n\\(a_j=(j,\\pi(j+1))\\).  \nHence the row interval of \\(R\\) contains the rows\n\\(i,i+1,\\dots ,j,j+1\\) and the column interval contains the two distinct\nhole‑columns \\(\\pi(i)\\) (the column of the hole in row \\(i\\)) and\n\\(\\pi(j+1)\\) (the column of the hole in row \\(j+1\\)).  \nBecause the row interval includes both rows \\(i\\) and \\(j+1\\), the rectangle\nalso contains the hole \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) or the hole \\((j+1,\\pi(j+1))\\),\ncontradicting the definition of a tile. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;1 each pair \\(\\{a_i,b_i\\}\\) forces the two cells to lie in\n**different** rectangles; from Lemma&nbsp;2 a single rectangle can intersect\nat most one of the \\((n-1)\\) sub‑grids.  Consequently the \\(2(n-1)\\) cells\n\\(\\{a_i,b_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) must be placed in at least \\(2(n-1)\\)\ndifferent rectangles.  Hence any tiling satisfies\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{number of tiles}\\ge 2n-2.}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nInequalities (1) and (3) coincide; therefore the minimum possible number\nof ti