 that \\(p(i_2)\\notin J\\).  \nThus the same alternative holds for all rows of \\(I\\); consequently \\(T\\subseteq L\\) or \\(T\\subseteq R\\). \u220e\n\nTherefore the total number of tiles equals\n\\[\nt=t_L+t_R,\n\\]\nwhere \\(t_L\\) (resp. \\(t_R\\)) is the number of tiles covering \\(L\\) (resp. \\(R\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound for \\(t_L\\)\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(i_j\\) be the unique row with \\(p(i_j)=j+1\\) (such a row exists because \\(p\\) is a permutation).  \nThe cell \\((i_j,j)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) while column \\(j+1\\) is a hole in that row.\n\n**Claim 2.** In any tiling of \\(L\\) the cell \\((i_j,j)\\) belongs to a rectangle whose rightmost column is exactly \\(j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(T\\) be the rectangle containing \\((i_j,j)\\).  \nIf the rightmost column of \\(T\\) were larger than \\(j\\), then column \\(j+1\\) would also belong to \\(T\\), contradicting the fact that \\((i_j,j+1)\\) is uncovered.  \nHence the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is at most \\(j\\).  \nSince column \\(j\\) must be covered, the rightmost column cannot be smaller than \\(j\\); therefore it equals \\(j\\). \u220e\n\nDistinct values of \\(j\\) give distinct rightmost columns, and a rectangle possesses a single rightmost column. Consequently at least one rectangle is required for each \\(j\\), i.e.\n\\[\nt_L\\ge n-1.\n\\]\n\nBy applying the same argument to the region \\(R\\) (or, equivalently, to the permutation \\(q(i)=n+1-p(i)\\)) we obtain\n\\[\nt_R\\ge n-1.\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible tiling satisfies\n\\[\nt=t_L+t_R\\ge 2(n-1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles  \n\nWe now exhibit a construction attaining the lower bound.\n\n**Tiling of \\(L\\).**  \nArrange the rows in increasing order of the values \\(p(i)\\).  \nFormally, let \\(\\sigma\\) be a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that\n\\[\np(\\sigma(1))<p(\\sigma(2))<\\dots <p(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\nSet \\(r_k=\\sigma(k)\\) for \\(k=1,\\dots ,n\\).  \nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define\n\\[\nk(j)=\\min\\{k\\mid p(r_k)>j\\}.\n\\]\nBecause the sequence \\(p(r_k)\\) is strictly increasing, the set \\(\\{k\\mid p(r_k)>j\\}\\) is a suffix \\(\\{k(j),k(j)+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nNow define the rectangle\n\\[\nR_j=\\{\\, (r_k,\\ell)\\mid k(j)\\le k\\le n,\\;1\\le\\ell\\le j\\,\\}.\n\\]\n*Verification.*  \nFor any row \\(r_k\\) with \\(k\\ge k(j)\\) we have \\(p(r_k)>j\\); thus columns \\(1,\\dots ,j\\) lie strictly left of the hole \\((r_k,p(r_k))\\), i.e. \\((r_k,\\ell)\\in L\\) for all \\(\\ell\\le j\\). Hence \\(R_j\\subseteq L\\).  \n\nThe rectangles \\(R_1,R_2,\\dots ,R_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint because their rightmost columns are distinct (the rightmost column of \\(R_j\\) is \\(j\\)).  \n\nFinally, let \\((i,j)\\in L\\). Then \\(j<p(i)\\). In the ordered list the row \\(i\\) appears as some \\(r_k\\) with \\(p(r_k)>j\\); consequently \\(k\\ge k(j)\\) and \\((i,j)=(r_k,j)\\in R_j\\). Hence the union of the \\(R_j\\) equals \\(L\\).  \nThus \\(L\\) is tiled by exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles, i.e. \\(t_L=n-1\\).\n\n**Tiling of \\(R\\).**  \nDefine the permutation \\(q(i)=n+1-p(i)\\).  \nA cell \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(R\\) iff \\(j>p(i)\\), equivalently \\(n+1-j<q(i)\\).  \nHence the region \\(R\\) is precisely the left region of the permutation \\(q\\).  \nApplying the construction above to \\(q\\) yields a tiling of \\(R\\) with exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles, i.e. \\(t_R=n-1\\).\n\nConsequently the whole board can be tiled with\n\\[\nt=t_L+t_R=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
