tly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n   Applying the same construction to the permutation \\(q(i)=n+1-p(i)\\) tiles \\(R\\) with another \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**  \n   The lower bound \\(2(n-1)\\) is attainable, so the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(c_i\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(i\\mapsto c_i\\) is a bijection of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); denote it by a permutation\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad p(i)=c_i .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  The two complementary regions  \n\nDefine\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j<p(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;p(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nThus the set of covered unit squares is exactly \\(L\\cup R\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n**Claim 1.** Every axis‑parallel rectangular tile that avoids all uncovered squares lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a rectangle \\(T\\) with rows \\(I=\\{a,a+1,\\dots ,b\\}\\) and columns \\(J=\\{c,c+1,\\dots ,d\\}\\).  \nFor a fixed row \\(i\\in I\\) the uncovered square is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nIf \\(c\\le p(i)\\le d\\) then \\((i,p(i))\\in T\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nHence for each \\(i\\in I\\) either \\(d<p(i)\\) (so \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,p(i)-1\\}\\)) or \\(c>p(i)\\) (so \\(J\\subseteq\\{p(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)).  \nIf both possibilities occurred for different rows of \\(I\\), then there would be rows \\(i_1,i_2\\in I\\) with\n\\(d<p(i_1)\\) and \\(c>p(i_2)\\); consequently \\(c\\le d<p(i_1)\\) and \\(c>p(i_2)\\), which forces\n\\(p(i_2)<c\\le d<p(i_1)\\).  \nSince the values \\(p(i)\\) are all distinct, the interval \\([c,d]\\) must contain the integer \\(p(i_2)\\), contradicting the fact that \\(p(i_2)\\notin J\\).  \nThus the same alternative holds for all rows of \\(I\\); consequently \\(T\\subseteq L\\) or \\(T\\subseteq R\\). ∎\n\nTherefore the total number of tiles equals\n\\[\nt=t_L+t_R,\n\\]\nwhere \\(t_L\\) (resp. \\(t_R\\)) is the number of tiles covering \\(L\\) (resp. \\(R\\)).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound for \\(t_L\\)\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(i_j\\) be the unique row with \\(p(i_j)=j+1\\) (such a row exists because \\(p\\) is a permutation).  \nThe cell \\((i_j,j)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) while column \\(j+1\\) is a hole in that row.\n\n**Claim 2.** In any tiling of \\(L\\) the cell \\((i_j,j)\\) belongs to a rectangle whose rightmost column is exactly \\(j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(T\\) be the rectangle containing \\((i_j,j)\\).  \nIf the rightmost column of \\(T\\) were larger than \\(j\\), then column \\(j+1\\) would also belong to \\(T\\), contradicting the fact that \\((i_j,j+1)\\) is uncovered.  \nHence the rightmost column of \\(T\\) is at most \\(j\\).  \nSince column \\(j\\) must be covered, the rightmost column cannot be smaller than \\(j\\); therefore it equals \\(j\\). ∎\n\nDistinct values of \\(j\\) give distinct rightmost columns, and a rectangle possesses a single rightmost column. Consequently at least one rectangle is required for each \\(j\\), i.e.\n\\[\nt_L\\ge n-1.\n\\]\n\nBy applying the same argument to the region \\(R\\) (or, equivalently, to the permutation \\(q(i)=n+1-p(i)\\)) we obtain\n\\[\nt_R\\ge n-1.\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible tiling satisfies\n\\[\nt=t_L+t_R\\ge 2(n-1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles  \n\nWe now exhibit a construction attaining the lower bound.\n\n**Tiling of \\(L\\).**  \nArrange the rows in increasing order of the values \\(p(i)\\).  \nFormally, let \\(\\sigma\\) be a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that\n\\[\np(\\sigma(1))<p(\\sigma(2))<\\dots <p(\\sigma(n)).\n\\]\nSet \\(r_k=\\sigma(k)\\) for \\(k=1,\\dots ,n\