lie in distinct rows) and together cover all squares except the \\(n\\) uncovered ones.  \n\nThus \\(n\\) tiles are both necessary and sufficient, giving the minimum \\(2025\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nIn each row and each column there must be exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nConsequently the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation  \n\\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDenote by \\(A\\) the \\(n\\times n\\) matrix whose entry \\(a_{ij}\\) equals  \n\n\\[\na_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n0,& (i,j)\\in U,\\\\[2pt]\n1,& (i,j)\\notin U .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A\\) records exactly the squares that must be covered by tiles.  \nLet \\(J\\) be the all‑ones matrix and let \\(P_\\pi\\) be the permutation matrix of \\(\\pi\\); i.e.  \n\n\\[\n(P_\\pi)_{ij}= \n\\begin{cases}\n1,& j=\\pi(i),\\\\\n0,&\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThen clearly  \n\n\\[\nA = J - P_\\pi . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Rank of \\(A\\)\n\nWe claim \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n\\).  \nConsider the linear system \\((J-P_\\pi)x=0\\).  \nSince \\(Jx = (\\sum_{k=1}^{n}x_k)\\mathbf 1\\) (where \\(\\mathbf 1\\) is the all‑ones column vector), the equation becomes  \n\n\\[\nP_\\pi x = s\\mathbf 1,\\qquad s:=\\sum_{k=1}^{n}x_k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe matrix \\(P_\\pi\\) merely permutes the coordinates of \\(x\\); therefore all entries of \\(P_\\pi x\\) are a permutation of the entries of \\(x\\). Equation (2) forces every entry of \\(P_\\pi x\\) – and hence every entry of \\(x\\) – to equal the same scalar \\(s\\). Thus \\(x=s\\mathbf 1\\).\n\nSubstituting \\(x=s\\mathbf 1\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\ns\\mathbf 1 = P_\\pi (s\\mathbf 1)=s\\mathbf 1,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds for any \\(s\\). Plugging \\(x=s\\mathbf 1\\) back into \\((J-P_\\pi)x\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n(J-P_\\pi)x = (n s - s)\\mathbf 1 = (n-1)s\\mathbf 1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n>1\\), the only solution of \\((J-P_\\pi)x=0\\) is \\(s=0\\), i.e. \\(x=0\\).  \nHence the nullspace of \\(J-P_\\pi\\) is trivial, so \\(J-P_\\pi\\) is invertible and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=\\operatorname{rank}(J-P_\\pi)=n . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Tiles as rank‑1 matrices\n\nA rectangular tile occupies a set of consecutive rows \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a set of consecutive columns \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nDefine the row‑indicator vector \\(u\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) by \\(u_i=1\\) iff \\(i\\in I\\), and the column‑indicator vector \\(v\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) by \\(v_j=1\\) iff \\(j\\in J\\).  \nThe tile covers precisely the entries of the rank‑1 matrix \\(uv^{\\!T}\\); all other entries of that matrix are zero.  \n\nIf a tiling uses \\(m\\) tiles, with indicator vectors \\((u^{(k)},v^{(k)})\\) for \\(k=1,\\dots ,m\\), then because the tiles are disjoint and together cover exactly the squares recorded by \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{m}u^{(k)}\\bigl(v^{(k)}\\bigr)^{\\!T}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach summand in (4) has rank at most \\(1\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\operatorname{rank}\\!\\bigl(u^{(k)}(v^{(k)})^{\\!T}\\bigr)\n\\le m . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (5) gives the lower bound  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge \\operatorname{rank}(A)=n . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling requires at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles\n\nFix the permutation \\(\\pi\\) that determines the uncovered squares.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place a tile \\(T_i\\) that occupies the whole row except the uncovered cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\); explicitly  \n\n\\[\nT_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(T_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle, the tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_n\\) lie in 