i)x = (n s - s)\\mathbf 1 = (n-1)s\\mathbf 1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n>1\\), the only solution of \\((J-P_\\pi)x=0\\) is \\(s=0\\), i.e. \\(x=0\\).  \nHence the nullspace of \\(J-P_\\pi\\) is trivial, so \\(J-P_\\pi\\) is invertible and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=\\operatorname{rank}(J-P_\\pi)=n . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Tiles as rank‑1 matrices\n\nA rectangular tile occupies a set of consecutive rows \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a set of consecutive columns \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nDefine the row‑indicator vector \\(u\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) by \\(u_i=1\\) iff \\(i\\in I\\), and the column‑indicator vector \\(v\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) by \\(v_j=1\\) iff \\(j\\in J\\).  \nThe tile covers precisely the entries of the rank‑1 matrix \\(uv^{\\!T}\\); all other entries of that matrix are zero.  \n\nIf a tiling uses \\(m\\) tiles, with indicator vectors \\((u^{(k)},v^{(k)})\\) for \\(k=1,\\dots ,m\\), then because the tiles are disjoint and together cover exactly the squares recorded by \\(A\\),\n\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{m}u^{(k)}\\bigl(v^{(k)}\\bigr)^{\\!T}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEach summand in (4) has rank at most \\(1\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{m}\\operatorname{rank}\\!\\bigl(u^{(k)}(v^{(k)})^{\\!T}\\bigr)\n\\le m . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (5) gives the lower bound  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge \\operatorname{rank}(A)=n . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible tiling requires at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles\n\nFix the permutation \\(\\pi\\) that determines the uncovered squares.  \nFor each row \\(i\\) place a tile \\(T_i\\) that occupies the whole row except the uncovered cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\); explicitly  \n\n\\[\nT_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq\\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(T_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle, the tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_n\\) lie in dist