h \\(i<j\\) (the part **above** the diagonal).  \n   - These are \\(n-1\\) tiles below the diagonal and \\(n-1\\) tiles above it; they are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square except the diagonal ones.  \n   - Hence the board can be tiled with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   - The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) holds for every admissible placement of uncovered squares, and the construction shows it is attainable.  \n   - For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation\n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square,\nthe set of uncovered squares is\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\nFor a fixed row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{\\,j\\mid 1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\,\\},\\qquad  \nR_i=\\{\\,j\\mid \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) (resp. \\(R_i\\)) is the set of columns to the **left** (resp. **right**) of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).\nThus the squares that must be covered are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(\\{i\\}\\times L_i\\;\\cup\\;\\{i\\}\\times R_i\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Every row forces a distinct tile on each side\n\nConsider a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\pi(i)>1\\); then \\(L_i\\neq\\varnothing\\) and its\nright\u2011most square is \\((i,\\pi(i)-1)\\).\nLet \\(T\\) be the tile that covers this square.\nSince a tile is a rectangle, its column interval is an integer interval\n\\([c,d]\\) with \\(c\\le d\\).\nBecause the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) is not covered,\n\\(d\\) cannot be \\(\\ge\\pi(i)\\); therefore \\(d\\le\\pi(i)-1\\).\nBut the tile contains the square \\((i,\\pi(i)-1)\\), so \\(d\\ge\\pi(i)-1\\).\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nd=\\pi(i)-1 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the **right side** of the tile covering the right\u2011most square of the left\npart of row \\(i\\) is forced to be the vertical grid line after column\n\\(\\pi(i)-1\\).\n\nSince \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, the numbers \\(\\pi(i)-1\\) (for those rows with\n\\(\\pi(i)>1\\)) are all distinct; they are precisely the set\n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\nHence the right\u2011most squares of the left parts of the \\(n-1\\) rows with\n\\(\\pi(i)>1\\) require **\\(n-1\\) different tiles**\u2014no single tile can serve two of\nthem, because a tile has only one right side and (1) shows that each such\nright side must be a different column.\n\nAn entirely symmetric argument works for the right parts.\nIf \\(\\pi(i)<n\\) then the left\u2011most square of the right part is\n\\((i,\\pi(i)+1)\\).  Any tile covering it must have its **left side** at column\n\\(\\pi(i)+1\\); the values \\(\\pi(i)+1\\) (for the \\(n-1\\) rows with\n\\(\\pi(i)<n\\)) are the distinct set \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\nThus the right parts also force **\\(n-1\\) distinct tiles**.\n\nSummarising,\n\n\\[\n\\text{any tiling uses at least }(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\text{ tiles}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the\ndiagonal \\((i,i)\\)).  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place a vertical tile\n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{below}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers the squares **below** the diagonal in column \\(j\\).\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a vertical tile\n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{above}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers the squares **above** the diagonal in column \\(j\\).\n\nThe tiles \\(T_j^{\\text{below}}\\) are pairwise disjoint, the tiles\n\\(T_j^{\\text{above}}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and a tile from the first family\nnever meets a tile from the second family (they lie on opposite sides of the\ndiagonal).  Together they cover every square of the board except the diagonal\nsquares \\((i,i)\\).  Their numbers are\n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{T_j^{\\text{below}}\\}\\,|=n-1,\\qquad\n|\\,\\{T_j^{\\text{above}}\\}\\,|=n-1,\n\\]\n\nso the total number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nThus the bound (2) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimal number for the given board\n\nFrom (2) any admissible tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the\nconstruction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{4048}\\)."
