\\) has a 0 on each diagonal entry (the uncovered squares) and a 1 elsewhere (the squares that must be covered). In compact notation\n\n\\[\nM = J - I,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(J\\) is the all\u2011ones matrix and \\(I\\) the identity matrix.\n\n#### 3. Tiles as rank\u20111 matrices\n\nA rectangular tile covering a set of rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a set of columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) occupies exactly the Cartesian product \\(R\\times C\\). Let\n\n\\[\nu_R\\in\\{0,1\\}^n,\\qquad (u_R)_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n1 & i\\in R,\\\\\n0 & i\\notin R,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nand define \\(v_C\\) analogously for \\(C\\). Then the indicator matrix of the tile is\n\n\\[\nB_{RC}=u_R v_C^{\\!\\top},\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\((B_{RC})_{ij}=1\\) iff \\(i\\in R\\) and \\(j\\in C\\), and \\(0\\) otherwise. Since \\(u_R\\) and \\(v_C\\) are non\u2011zero vectors, \\(B_{RC}\\) has rank\u202f1 (it is an outer product of two non\u2011zero vectors).\n\nBecause no tile may contain an uncovered square, after diagonalisation we must have\n\n\\[\nR\\cap C=\\varnothing . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if some index \\(i\\) belonged to both \\(R\\) and \\(C\\), the tile would cover the diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\), which is forbidden.\n\nLet the collection of tiles be \\(\\{B_{R_tC_t}\\}_{t=1}^{k}\\), where \\(k\\) is the number of tiles. The tiles are pairwise disjoint and together cover precisely the off\u2011diagonal cells. Hence\n\n\\[\nM = \\sum_{t=1}^{k} B_{R_tC_t}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n#### 4. Lower bound via matrix rank\n\nFor any matrices \\(A,B\\) over \\(\\mathbb{R}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A+B)\\le \\operatorname{rank}(A)+\\operatorname{rank}(B).\n\\]\n\nApplying this repeatedly to (2) yields\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\le \\sum_{t=1}^{k}\\operatorname{rank}(B_{R_tC_t}) = \\sum_{t=1}^{k} 1 = k. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(k\\ge \\operatorname{rank}(M)\\).\n\nWe compute \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)\\). The eigenvalues of the all\u2011ones matrix \\(J\\) are \\(n\\) (with eigenvector \\(\\mathbf{1}=(1,\\dots ,1)^{\\!\\top}\\)) and \\(0\\) with multiplicity \\(n-1\\). Subtracting the identity shifts each eigenvalue by \\(-1\\). Consequently the eigenvalues of \\(M=J-I\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_1=n-1,\\qquad \\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=\\dots =\\lambda_n=-1 .\n\\]\n\nAll eigenvalues are non\u2011zero, so \\(M\\) is invertible and\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (3) and (4) we obtain the lower bound\n\n\\[\nk\\ge n = 2025. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### 5. Construction achieving the bound\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n\\)) define the tile\n\n\\[\nT_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq i\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle covering all squares of row \\(i\\) except the diagonal square \\((i,i)\\). The family \\(\\{T_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) consists of \\(n\\) pairwise disjoint tiles, because each tile occupies a distinct row.\n\n*Verification of the required properties*:\n\n- **Coverage of off\u2011diagonal cells.**  \n  Let \\((i,j)\\) be any cell with \\(i\\neq j\\). Since column \\(j\\) belongs to the column set of \\(T_i\\) (all columns except \\(i\\)), the cell \\((i,j)\\) lies in \\(T_i\\). Hence every off\u2011diagonal cell is covered exactly once.\n\n- **Uncovered squares.**  \n  For a diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\), column \\(i\\) is omitted from the column set of \\(T_i\\); no other tile uses row \\(i\\). Therefore \\((i,i)\\) is not covered by any tile. Consequently each row and each column contain exactly one uncovered square, namely the diagonal cell of that row/column.\n\nThus the configuration satisfies all conditions and uses exactly \\(n=2025\\) tiles.\n\n#### 6. Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound (5) shows that any admissible tiling requires at least \\(2025\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2025\\) tiles suffice. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\n1. **Normalization check:** Verify that permuting rows and columns to place holes on the diagonal does not alter the tile count; the bijection is one\u2011to\u2011one and preserves rectangular shapes.\n\n2. **Matrix\u2011tile correspondence:** Confirm that each rectangular tile corresponds to an outer product \\(u_R v_C^{\\!\\top}\\) and thus has rank\u202f1. Ensure condition (1) (\\(R\\cap C=\\varnothing\\)) correctly encodes \u201cno uncovered square lies inside the tile\u201d.\n\n3. **Rank computation:** Re\u2011derive eigenvalues of \\(J\\) and of \\(J-I\\) to guarantee \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M)=n\\). Check that no eigenvalue is zero, confirming invertibility.\n\n4. **Rank inequality:** Re\u2011affirm subadditivity of rank over \\(\\mathbb{R}\\): \\(\\operatorname{rank}(A+B)\\le\\operatorname{rank}(A)+\\operatorname{rank}(B)\\). Apply iteratively to the sum of tile matrices.\n\n5. **Construction verification:** For any off\u2011diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\), show explicitly that it belongs to tile \\(T_i\\); for any diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\), show it belongs to no tile. This constitutes a point\u2011by\u2011point verification in a general form.\n\n6. **Edge cases:** Ensure that tiles of size \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) are allowed by the problem statement (they are rectangular with sides on grid lines).\n\n7. **Conclusion consistency:** Confirm that the lower bound and the explicit construction match, establishing optimality.\n\nAfter confirming all points, the solution is complete and free of gaps."
