al rectangle  \n     \\[\n     H_c=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<c,\\;j\\ge c\\},\n     \\]\n     which lies entirely to the right of the holes in its rows.  \n   The families \\(\\{V_c\\}\\) and \\(\\{H_c\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, together they cover every non‑diagonal square exactly once, and there are \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the unit squares of the \\(n\\times n\\) board by ordered pairs \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\) (row \\(i\\), column \\(j\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square,\nthere exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis precisely the set of uncovered squares.  All other squares must be\ncovered by rectangular tiles whose sides lie on grid lines, and no two\ntiles may overlap.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A fractional‑packing lower bound  \n\nFor a row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\qquad\\text{(if }\\sigma(i)>1\\text{)} ,\n\\]\n\\[\nR_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\qquad\\text{(if }\\sigma(i)<n\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) is the square immediately to the **left** of the hole in row \\(i\\);\n\\(R_i\\) is the square immediately to the **right** of that hole.\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{L_i\\mid\\sigma(i)>1\\}\\cup\\{R_i\\mid\\sigma(i)<n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(2n-2\\) squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2.1  \nLet \\(R=I\\times J\\) be a rectangle of uncovered squares (hence it contains\nno hole).  Then\n\n* (a) \\(R\\) contains **at most one** left‑hand neighbour \\(L_i\\);\n* (b) \\(R\\) contains **at most one** right‑hand neighbour \\(R_i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(R\\) contains two distinct left neighbours \\(L_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) and\n\\(L_{i'}=(i',\\sigma(i')-1)\\) with \\(i<i'\\).\nBoth column numbers \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i')-1\\) belong to the column\ninterval \\(J\\); because \\(J\\) is an interval of integers, it also contains\nevery integer between them, in particular \\(\\sigma(i')\\).\nSince \\(i'\\in I\\), the hole \\((i',\\sigma(i'))\\) would lie inside \\(R\\),\ncontradicting the definition of a tile.  Hence (a) holds.\nThe argument for (b) is identical, using the squares \\(R_i\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2.2 (fractional packing)  \nAssign weight  \n\n\\[\nw(L_i)=w(R_i)=\\tfrac12\\qquad\\text{for every }i\\text{ for which the square exists},\n\\]\nand weight \\(0\\) to all other uncovered squares.\nThen for any rectangle \\(R\\) of uncovered squares,\n\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{z\\in R} w(z)\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 2.1, \\(R\\) contains at most one left neighbour and at most one\nright neighbour.  Each such square contributes weight \\(\\tfrac12\\); therefore\nthe total weight contributed by \\(R\\) is at most \\(\\tfrac12+\\tfrac12=1\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2.3 (lower bound)  \nAny tiling of the board satisfying the problem’s conditions uses at least\n\\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe total weight of all squares in \\(\\mathcal N\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal N} w(z)=\\frac12\\cdot(2n-2)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nEvery tile is a rectangle of uncovered squares, and by Lemma 2.2 its weight\ncontribution is at most \\(1\\).  Hence covering all of \\(\\mathcal N\\) requires\nat least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.  Since the tiles must also cover the other\nuncovered squares, any admissible tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).\nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).\n\n*Left‑side rectangles.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid i>c\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_c\\) is a vertical rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\); for every row\n\\(i>c\\) we have \\(\\sigma(i)=i>c\\), so the hole in that row lies to the\nright of column \\(c\\).  Consequently \\(V_c\\) contains only squares left of\nthe holes and is a legal tile.\n\n*Right‑side rectangles.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_c=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<c,\\;j\\ge c\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_c\\) is a horizontal rectangle of size \\((c-1)\\times (n-c+1)\\); for every\nrow \\(i<c\\) we have \\(\\sigma(i)=i<c\\), so the hole in that row lies to the\nleft of column \\(c\\).  Hence \\(H_c\\) consists solely of squares right of the\nholes.\n\n*Disjointness and completeness.*  \nThe sets \\(\\{V_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy\ndifferent columns.  The sets \\(\\{H_c\\}_{c=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint\nbecause they occupy different column intervals.  Moreover, a vertical rectangle\n\\(V_c\\) uses rows \\(i>c\\) while a horizontal rectangle \\(H_c\\) uses rows\n\\(i<c\\); therefore a vertical and a horizontal rectangle never intersect.\nConsequently the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\nEvery square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\) belongs either to a vertical rectangle\n(\\(j<i\\)) or to a horizontal rectangle (\\(j>i\\)), so all non‑diagonal squares\nare covered exactly once.  The diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered,\nas required.\n\nThus we have exhibited a tiling using precisely  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimality and final answer  \n\nLemma 2.3 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the\nconstruction above achieves this bound.  Hence the minimum possible number\nof tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
