tely in \\(R\\), and the family \\(\\{T_j^{\\text{R}}\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) is pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n- The two families are disjoint because every cell of a left‑side tile satisfies column \\(j<\\) row, whereas every cell of a right‑side tile satisfies column \\(> \\) row.  \n- Together they cover every cell of \\(L\\) and \\(R\\): for a fixed column \\(j\\) the cells of \\(L\\) are precisely \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i>j\\), which are exactly the cells of \\(T_j^{\\text{L}}\\); similarly the cells of \\(R\\) in column \\(k=n-j+1\\) are \\((i,k)\\) with \\(i<k\\), which are exactly the cells of \\(T_j^{\\text{R}}\\).  \n- No tile contains an uncovered diagonal cell, because a left‑side tile uses only columns \\(j\\le n-1\\) and rows \\(i\\ge j+1>j\\); a right‑side tile uses only columns \\(k\\ge2\\) and rows \\(i\\le k-1<k\\). Hence the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) are never covered.\n\nThus we have constructed a tiling with exactly  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof  \n\nLemma 1 shows that any admissible tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while Lemma 2 exhibits a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nHence the lower bound is tight, and the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore the minimum number of rectangular tiles that Matilda must place is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
