\\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying \u201cexactly one uncovered square in each row and each column\u2019\u2019 determines a set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the uncovered squares are precisely the points of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Reduction to the main diagonal  \n\nPermuting the columns of the board by the bijection \\(j\\mapsto p^{-1}(j)\\) sends the set \\(U\\) to the main diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines is mapped to a rectangle of the same size, and the property \u201cno rectangle contains a point of \\(U\\)\u201d is preserved. Hence a tiling of the board with uncovered set \\(U\\) using \\(t\\) tiles exists iff a tiling with uncovered set \\(D\\) using the same number \\(t\\) of tiles exists. Consequently it suffices to treat the case  \n\n\\[\nU=D.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Geometry of admissible rectangles  \n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n\\}\\qquad\\text{(strictly above the diagonal)},\n\\]\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le n\\}\\qquad\\text{(strictly below the diagonal)}.\n\\]\n\nA rectangle is a product \\(R=I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are intervals of rows and columns, respectively.  \n\n**Lemma\u202f1.** If \\(R\\cap D=\\varnothing\\) then either \\(R\\subseteq A\\) or \\(R\\subseteq B\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose \\(R=I\\times J\\) contains a cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) (so \\((i,j)\\in A\\)) and a cell \\((k,\\ell)\\) with \\(k>\\ell\\) (so \\((k,\\ell)\\in B\\)). Then \\(i\\le k\\) and \\(\\ell\\le j\\); consequently the diagonal cell \\((r,r)\\) with \\(r\\in[I]\\cap[J]\\) lies in \\(R\\), contradicting \\(R\\cap D=\\varnothing\\). Hence all cells of \\(R\\) lie on the same side of the diagonal. \u220e\n\nThus any admissible rectangle is wholly contained in \\(A\\) or wholly contained in \\(B\\). The two regions can be tiled independently, and the total number of tiles equals the sum of the numbers used for \\(A\\) and for \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Lower bound for the upper\u2011triangular region  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nS^{+}=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subseteq A,\n\\]\n\nthe *super\u2011diagonal* cells.  \n\n**Lemma\u202f2.** No two distinct cells of \\(S^{+}\\) can belong to the same rectangle contained in \\(A\\).\n\n*Proof.* Let \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((k,k+1)\\) be two cells of \\(S^{+}\\) with \\(i<k\\). Any rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\subseteq A\\) that contains both must satisfy  \n\n\\[\ni,k\\in I,\\qquad i+1,k+1\\in J.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(I\\) contains the interval \\([i,k]\\) and \\(J\\) contains \\([i+1,k+1]\\). The diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) belongs to the product \\([i,k]\\times[i+1,k+1]\\), contradicting \\(R\\subseteq A\\). \u220e\n\nSince the rectangles covering \\(A\\) are pairwise disjoint and must cover every cell of \\(S^{+}\\), Lemma\u202f2 forces at least \\(|S^{+}|=n-1\\) rectangles to be used for \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lower bound for the lower\u2011triangular region  \n\nBy symmetry, the *sub\u2011diagonal*  \n\n\\[\nS^{-}=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subseteq B\n\\]\n\nhas the same property: any two of its cells cannot lie in the same rectangle contained in \\(B\\). Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Global lower bound  \n\nCombining the two sides we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{any tiling of }[n]\\times[n]\\setminus D\\text{ uses at least }(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\text{ rectangles.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. A tiling attaining the bound  \n\nDefine the following families of rectangles.\n\n*Upper region.* For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nR^{+}_{j}= \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R^{+}_{j}\\) is a vertical rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\). The collection \\(\\{R^{+}_{j}\\mid j=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) rectangles, they are pairwise disjoint, and their union is exactly \\(A\\).\n\n*Lower region.* For each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nR^{-}_{i}= \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(R^{-}_{i}\\) is a horizontal rectangle of size \\(1\\times(i-1)\\). The collection \\(\\{R^{-}_{i}\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) rectangles, they are pairwise disjoint, and their union is exactly \\(B\\).\n\nAll rectangles together cover every cell of the board except the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), which remain uncovered. Hence we have a tiling with exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Minimality  \n\nInequality (1) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient. Therefore the minimal possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8. Numerical answer  \n\nFor the original problem \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nThus Matilda can achieve the required condition with \\(4048\\) tiles, and it is impossible to do it with fewer.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
