y have any integer side lengths, and no two tiles may overlap (each unit square is covered by at most one tile).  \nExactly one unit square in each row and each column must remain uncovered.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to the diagonal case  \n\nLet the uncovered squares be \\(\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) where \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nPermuting the columns by the permutation \\(\\sigma^{-1}\\) sends the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) from column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) to column \\(i\\).  \nSince a column permutation merely relabels the columns, the existence of a tiling with a certain number of tiles is unchanged.  \nHence **without loss of generality** we may assume  \n\n\\[\n\\text{the uncovered squares are }(i,i)\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The board splits into two disjoint triangles  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n\\},\\qquad\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(U\\) is the strictly upper‑triangular part of the board, \\(L\\) the strictly lower‑triangular part; together they are exactly the set of squares not containing a hole, by (1).  \n\n**Claim.** Every admissible rectangle is contained wholly in \\(U\\) or wholly in \\(L\\).  \n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contains a square \\((i_1,j_1)\\in U\\) (so \\(i_1<j_1\\)) and a square \\((i_2,j_2)\\in L\\) (so \\(i_2>j_2\\)).  \nBecause a rectangle consists of a Cartesian product of a set of rows and a set of columns, we have  \n\n\\[\ni_1\\le i_2,\\qquad j_2\\le j_1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the cell \\((j_2,j_2)\\) lies in the same row interval and column interval as \\((i_1,j_1)\\) and \\((i_2,j_2)\\); hence \\((j_2,j_2)\\) belongs to \\(R\\).  \nBut \\((j_2,j_2)\\) is a diagonal square, i.e. a hole, contradicting the definition of a tile. ∎  \n\nThus the tiling of the whole board is the disjoint union of a tiling of \\(U\\) and a tiling of \\(L\\).  \nBecause the two regions are symmetric, it suffices to bound the number of rectangles needed for \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the upper‑triangular region  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subseteq U . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S\\) lie on the “first column” of each row of \\(U\\); they are pairwise in distinct rows and distinct columns.\n\n**Lemma.** No admissible rectangle can contain two distinct points of \\(S\\).  \n\n*Proof.* Let \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((k,k+1)\\) be two distinct points of \\(S\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nAny rectangle containing both must contain all rows \\(i,\\dots ,k\\) and all columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,k+1\\).  \nSince \\(i+1\\le k\\), the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) belongs to that rectangle.  \nBut \\((i+1,i+1)\\) is a hole, contradicting admissibility. ∎  \n\nBecause each rectangle covers at most one element of \\(S\\) and \\(|S|=n-1\\), any partition of \\(U\\) into rectangles needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\nThe same argument applied to the lower‑triangular region \\(L\\) (using the symmetric set \\(\\{(i+1,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for \\(L\\) as well.\n\nHence **any tiling of the whole board uses at least**\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nrectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling attaining the bound  \n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal as in (1).  \nFor each column \\(j\\) we tile the squares of that column which are not holes:\n\n* If \\(j=1\\) (the hole is at the top of the column), use the single vertical rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  R_1=\\{(i,1)\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}.\n  \\]\n\n* If \\(j=n\\) (the hole is at the bottom), use  \n\n  \\[\n  R_n=\\{(i,n)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n  \\]\n\n* For every intermediate column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n-1\\) the hole lies at \\((j,j)\\); we split the column into two vertical rectangles  \n\n  \\[\n  R_j^{\\text{top}}   =\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n  \\qquad\n  R_j^{\\text{bot}}   =\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n  \\]\n\nAll these rectangles are axis‑parallel, consist of contiguous rows, and none contains a diagonal square.  \nThey are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square of the board except the diagonal ones.  \n\nCounting the rectangles:\n\n* one rectangle for column \\(1\\);\n* one rectangle for column \\(n\\);\n* two rectangles for each of the remaining \\(n-2\\) columns.\n\nHence the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n1+1+2(n-2)=2n-2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles, and the explicit construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
