tinct tile. Similarly for right side. So lower bound 2n-2 holds.\n\nBut we need to verify that the left side and right side tile sets are disjoint. Could a tile be both left side tile for one row and right side tile for another? Let's see: left side tile has rightmost column L_i = pi(i)-1. Right side tile has leftmost column pi(j)+1. Could a tile have both rightmost column = pi(i)-1 and leftmost column = pi(j)+1? That would require pi(i)-1 >= pi(j)+1 (since leftmost <= rightmost). But then tile would include column pi(i) (since pi(i) = L_i+1) which is a hole in row i, unless tile does not include row i. But tile could be in a different row. So tile could be a rectangle covering rows other than i and j. But the tile's column interval would be from pi(j)+1 to pi(i)-1. Could that be non-empty? Possibly if pi(i)-1 >= pi(j)+1. Then tile's column interval excludes columns pi(i) and pi(j) (holes) because they are outside the interval? Actually pi(i) = L_i+1 = pi(i)-1+1 = pi(i). So pi(i) is one column to the right of L_i. Since tile's rightmost column is L_i, tile does not include column pi(i). Similarly, tile's leftmost column is pi(j)+1, so tile does not include column pi(j). So tile could be a rectangle covering rows other than i and j. But the tile must also cover the cell (i, L_i) for left side tile, and cell (j, pi(j)+1) for right side tile. But a single tile cannot cover both cells because they are in different rows (i and j) and the tile would need to include both rows. That's possible: tile could be multi-row covering rows i and j. But then the tile's column interval must include both columns L_i and pi(j)+1. If L_i < pi(j)+1, then tile's column interval must start at L_i (or earlier) and end at pi(j)+1 (or later). But then tile would include column pi(i) (since pi(i) = L_i+1) if L_i+1 <= pi(j)+1? Actually if L_i < pi(j)+1, then pi(i) = L_i+1 <= pi(j)+1, so column pi(i) is within tile's column interval, which would include hole (i, pi(i)). That violates condition. 