                "content": ".\n\n**### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:** I have found a complete solution. The minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix.**  \n   Since each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a set \\(\\{(i,f(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) where \\(f\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (here \\(n=2025\\)).  \n\n2. **Reduction to the diagonal case.**  \n   By permuting the columns according to \\(f^{-1}\\) we may assume that the uncovered squares are the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\). Column permutations preserve the property “each side of a tile lies on a grid line’’ and keep the number of tiles unchanged.  \n\n3. **Upper bound – a construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles.**  \n   For each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1)\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells \\((i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\).  \n   For each column \\(j\\;(1\\le j\\le n-1)\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\).  \n   The diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) remain uncovered; each row and each column has exactly one uncovered cell.  \n   The construction uses \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.  \n\n4. **Lower bound – every tile can cover at most one of a special set of squares.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the squares immediately to the right of each diagonal hole and immediately below each diagonal hole.  \n   \\(|S|=2n-2\\).  \n\n   **Claim.** Any axis‑parallel rectangle that avoids all diagonal holes contains **at most one** element of \\(S\\).  \n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Let a rectangle be determined by a consecutive set of rows \\(R=[a,b]\\) and a consecutive set of columns \\(C=[c,d]\\).  \n   Suppose it contains two distinct squares of \\(S\\).  \n\n   *Case 1.* Both belong to \\(\\{(i,i+1)\\}\\); say \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \n   Then \\(a\\le i<j\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le i+1<j+1\\le d\\). Consequently the square \\((i+1,i+1)\\) (a diagonal hole) lies in the rectangle, a contradiction.  \n\n   *Case 2.* Both belong to \\(\\{(i+1,i)\\}\\); the argument is symmetric and forces the hole \\((i+1,i+1)\\) to lie inside the rectangle.  \n\n   *Case 3.* One square is \\((i,i+1)\\) and the other is \\((j+1,j)\\).  \n   Without loss of generality \\(i\\le j\\). Then rows \\(i\\) and \\(j+1\\) are in \\(R\\) and columns \\(i+1\\) and \\(j\\) are in \\(C\\).  \n   Hence the square \\((i+1,i+1)\\) (again a diagonal hole) belongs to the rectangle, a contradiction.  \n\n   In every case a second element of \\(S\\) forces a diagonal hole to be covered, which is forbidden. ∎  \n\n   Because each tile is a rectangle, the claim shows that **each tile can cover at most one element of \\(S\\)**. Since all squares of \\(S\\) must be covered (they are not holes), any tiling uses at least \\(|S|=2n-2\\) tiles.  \n\n5. **Conclusion.** The construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient, and the claim shows that no tiling can use fewer. Hence the minimum number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\nThus the required minimum number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).  "
