sion for the whole board:**  \n   - The lower triangle is symmetric to the upper triangle, so it also requires exactly \\(n-1\\) rectangles.  \n   - Therefore the whole \\(n\\times n\\) board needs \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles.\n\n6. **Plugging \\(n=2025\\):**  \n   \\[\n     2(2025-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048.\n   \\]  \n\nThus the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the unit squares of the board by ordered pairs \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\), where \\(i\\) is the row index and \\(j\\) the column index.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Reduction to a diagonal configuration  \n\nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely \\(\\{(i,p(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).\n\nPermuting the columns of the whole board by the permutation \\(p^{-1}\\) sends the uncovered\nsquares to the main diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).  \nA tiling of the board after this column permutation corresponds bijectively (by applying the inverse column permutation) to a tiling before the permutation, and the number of tiles is unchanged.  \nConsequently we may **assume without loss of generality** that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Decomposition into upper and lower triangles  \n\nDefine  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n\\},\\qquad\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nEvery unit square not on the diagonal belongs either to \\(U\\) (above the diagonal) or to \\(L\\) (below the diagonal).\n\nLet a tile be a rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and\n\\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are intervals of consecutive integers.\nIf \\(R\\) contained a square of \\(U\\) and a square of \\(L\\), then it would also contain a diagonal square \\((k,k)\\) for some \\(k\\in I\\cap J\\), contradicting the requirement that diagonal squares stay uncovered.  \nHence **every tile lies entirely in \\(U\\) or entirely in \\(L\\)**.\n\nBecause the board is symmetric with respect to the main diagonal, the minimal number of tiles needed for \\(U\\) equals that for \\(L\\).  \nLet \\(g(n)\\) denote the minimal number of rectangles required to partition \\(U\\).  \nThe total minimal number of tiles for the whole board is therefore \\(2g(n)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A lower bound for \\(g(n)\\)\n\nConsider the set of cells immediately above the diagonal:\n\\[\nD=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\subseteq U .\n\\]\n\n**Lemma.** No rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\) (with \\(I,J\\) intervals) can contain two distinct cells of \\(D\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume \\(R\\) contains \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((i',i'+1)\\) with \\(i<i'\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) contains both rows \\(i\\) and \\(i'\\), the row interval is \\(I=[a,b]\\) with \\(a\\le i<i'\\le b\\); thus \\(b\\ge i'\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) contains both columns \\(i+1\\) and \\(i'+1\\), the column interval is \\(J=[c,d]\\) with \\(c\\le i+1<i'+1\\le d\\); thus \\(c\\le i+1\\).  \n\nA rectangle lying in \\(U\\) must avoid the diagonal, which forces the column interval to start strictly to the right of the bottom row index:\n\\[\nc>b .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom the inequalities above we have\n\\[\nc\\le i+1\\le i' \\le b ,\n\\]\nwhich contradicts (1). \u220e\n\nSince the \\(n-1\\) cells of \\(D\\) must belong to distinct rectangles, any partition of \\(U\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. Hence\n\n\\[\ng(n)\\ge n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  An upper bound (explicit construction)\n\nFor each \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the rectangle\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\nGeometrically, \\(R_i\\) is the whole \\(i\\)-th row of the upper triangle.  \nThe rectangles \\(R_1,R_2,\\dots ,R_{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, a