ells is  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nand the set of cells that have to be tiled is the complement \\(C=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Decomposition of the covered set  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\nThus \\(L_i\\) is the block of cells **left** of the hole in row \\(i\\) and \\(R_i\\) the block **right** of it.  \nSet\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\nClearly \\(C=L\\cup R\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   A lower bound for the left part  \n\nFix a column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\).  \nA cell of column \\(c\\) belongs to \\(L\\) precisely when its row \\(i\\) satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)>c\\).  \nDefine\n\\[\nB_c=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)>c\\,\\}.\n\\]\nThe set \\(B_c\\) is a subset of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); in a tiling a rectangle can contain column \\(c\\) only together with a *consecutive* set of rows, i.e. a set of the form \\(\\{a,a+1,\\dots ,b\\}\\).  \nHence the cells of column \\(c\\) that lie in \\(L\\) must be covered by at least as many rectangles as there are maximal consecutive blocks of \\(B_c\\).  \n\nLet \\(b_c\\) denote the number of such blocks. Then any tiling uses at least \\(b_c\\) rectangles covering column \\(c\\). Summing over all columns,\n\\begin{equation}\n\\label{eq:leftlower}\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}b_c .\n\\end{equation}\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Computing \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}b_c\\)\n\nWrite \\(p_i=\\sigma(i)\\) and set \\(p_0=0\\).  \nScanning the rows from top to bottom, a new block of rows for a fixed column \\(c\\) starts exactly when we encounter a row \\(i\\) with \\(p_i>c\\) while the preceding row sati