 is  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nand the set of cells that have to be tiled is the complement \\(C=[n]\\times[n]\\setminus U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Decomposition of the covered set  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad   \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\nThus \\(L_i\\) is the block of cells **left** of the hole in row \\(i\\) and \\(R_i\\) the block **right** of it.  \nSet\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i,\\qquad R=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}R_i .\n\\]\nClearly \\(C=L\\cup R\\) and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   A lower bound for the left part  \n\nFix a column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\).  \nA cell of column \\(c\\) belongs to \\(L\\) precisely when its row \\(i\\) satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)>c\\).  \nDefine\n\\[\nB_c=\\{\\,i\\mid \\sigma(i)>c\\,\\}.\n\\]\nThe set \\(B_c\\) is a subset of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); in a tiling a rectangle can contain column \\(c\\) only together with a *consecutive* set of rows, i.e. a set of the form \\(\\{a,a+1,\\dots ,b\\}\\).  \nHence the cells of column \\(c\\) that lie in \\(L\\) must be covered by at least as many rectangles as there are maximal consecutive blocks of \\(B_c\\).  \n\nLet \\(b_c\\) denote the number of such blocks. Then any tiling uses at least \\(b_c\\) rectangles covering column \\(c\\). Summing over all columns,\n\\begin{equation}\n\\label{eq:leftlower}\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}b_c .\n\\end{equation}\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Computing \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}b_c\\)\n\nWrite \\(p_i=\\sigma(i)\\) and set \\(p_0=0\\).  \nScanning the rows from top to bottom, a new block of rows for a fixed column \\(c\\) starts exactly when we encounter a row \\(i\\) with \\(p_i>c\\) while the preceding row satisfies \\(p_{i-1}\\le c\\).  \nConsequently,\n\\[\nb_c=\\#\\{\\,i\\mid p_i>c\\text{ and }(i=1\\text{ or }p_{i-1}\\le c)\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nSumming over all \\(c\\) and interchanging the order of summation gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}b_c\n= \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\bigl(p_i-\\max\\{p_{i-1},0\\}\\bigr)_+\n= p_1-1+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\bigl(p_i-p_{i-1},0\\bigr),\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nwhere \\((x)_+=\\max\\{x,0\\}\\).\n\nBecause \\((p_1,\\dots ,p_n)\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the quantity on the right–hand side of (4.1) is minimized when the sequence is monotone (either increasing or decreasing).  \nFor a monotone sequence one has\n\\[\np_1-1+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max(p_i-p_{i-1},0)=n-1 .\n\\]\nIf the permutation is not monotone, at least one term \\(\\max(p_i-p_{i-1},0)\\) exceeds the value it would have in a monotone sequence, and the sum becomes strictly larger than \\(n-1\\).  \nThus\n\\begin{equation}\n\\label{eq:leftbound}\n\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}b_c\\;\\ge\\;n-1,\n\\end{equation}\nwith equality exactly for monotone permutations.\n\nCombining \\eqref{eq:leftlower} and \\eqref{eq:leftbound} we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }L\\;\\ge\\;n-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   A symmetric bound for the right part  \n\nThe same argument applied to the rows (instead of the columns) yields\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{rectangles covering }R\\;\\ge\\;n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.   Global lower bound  \n\nSince the tiles covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\) are disjoint,\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.   Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the **identity permutation** \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n\n*Vertical tiles.* For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(V_c\\) is a \\( (n-c)\\times1\\) tile; it covers precisely the cells of column \\(c\\) that lie **below** the diagonal.\n\n*Horizontal tiles.* For each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle\n\\[\nH_r=\\{(r,j)\\mid j=r+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\\(H_r\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-r)\\) tile; it covers precisely the cells of row \\(r\\) that lie **to the right** of the diagonal.\n\n*Verification.*  \n- Every tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.  \n- The vertical tiles occupy distinct columns, the horizontal tiles distinct rows; therefore any two different tiles are disjoint.  \n- Let \\((i,j)\\) be a cell with \\(i\\neq j\\).  \n  *If \\(i<j\\)* then \\(j>\\sigma(i)=i\\) and \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\).  \n  *If \\(i>j\\)* then \\(j<\\sigma(i)=i\\) and \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\).  \n  Hence each covered cell belongs to exactly one tile, while the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) are uncovered.  \n\nThe number of tiles is\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{vertical}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{horizontal}}=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 8.   Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound (7) and the explicit construction both give the same value.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
