\).\n\n4. **Lower bound for the right part.**  \n   The argument is symmetric. Let \\(s\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(s)=1\\).  \n   In that row the cells \\((s,2),\\dots ,(s,n)\\) belong to \\(R\\); column \\(2\\) appears only in this row, so a single rectangle confined to row \\(s\\) must cover all those cells. Removing row \\(s\\) and column \\(1\\) yields an \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\) instance to which the induction hypothesis applies, giving at least \\((n-2)\\) further rectangles for the reduced right part. Hence at least \\((n-2)+1=n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(R\\).\n\n5. **Global lower bound.**  \n   Since the tiles covering \\(L\\) and those covering \\(R\\) are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles.\n\n6. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n   - For each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n     \\[\n     V_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     a \\((n-c)\\times1\\) rectangle.  \n   - For each row \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal tile  \n\n     \\[\n     H_r=\\{(r,j)\\mid j=r+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     a \\(1\\times (n-r)\\) rectangle.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   \u2022 Every tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.  \n   \u2022 The vertical tiles occupy distinct columns, the horizontal tiles distinct rows; therefore any two different tiles are disjoint.  \n   \u2022 If a cell \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(i<j\\) then \\(j>\\sigma(i)=i\\) and \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\).  \n     If \\(i>j\\) then \\(j<\\sigma(i)=i\\) and \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\).  \n     Hence every cell not on the diagonal belongs to exactly one tile, while the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) are uncovered.  \n   \u2022 The number of tiles is \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same value; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
